Yes—structurally, a lot of the same machinery applies.

Santa is almost a miniature laboratory for thinking about gods: an unseen agent, testimony from trusted adults, ritualized behavior, moral surveillance, rewards/punishments, culturally inherited stories, symbolic representations, apparent “evidence,” communities participating in the narrative, and eventually the question of whether the story points to a literal entity, a social mechanism, an archetype, or several things at once.

The important move is that “Santa isn’t literally a man flying around the planet” does not imply “Santa is nothing.” Santa exists as a cultural object, behavioral force, economic phenomenon, narrative pattern, role instantiated by many people, and symbol of generosity. The abstraction can survive the collapse of the naïve ontology.

You can run the same decomposition on gods:

literal being → reported encounters → tradition → institution → ritual → psychological representation → social coordination mechanism → archetype → metaphysical proposition → underlying generator

And then ask separately at every layer: What exactly is being claimed to exist? What observations would discriminate between explanations? What remains true if the most literal interpretation fails?

There is one major difference, though. Santa makes relatively specific empirical claims—North Pole workshop, reindeer, one-night global delivery—that collide pretty directly with ordinary physical evidence. “God” can denote claims ranging from a Zeus-like supernatural person to the ground of being, necessary existence, consciousness, Logos, ultimate reality, or simply a name placed over whatever generates existence. Those are radically different hypotheses and cannot all be tested or dismissed in the same way.

So the rabbit hole gets interesting precisely where the Santa analogy breaks.

You can discover that a childhood representation was false without thereby settling whether the thing that representation was trying to compress has a deeper referent.

In Pantheon language: do not mistake failure of one interface for failure of the underlying generator. But also don’t assume an underlying generator merely because the interface is powerful. Strip the costume off, perform the echoectomy, and see what independently regenerates.